<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francesco Novello da Carrara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1359–1406</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francesco Novello da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a> was the last Carrara lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-galeazzo-visconti">Gian Galeazzo Visconti</a> took Padua in 1388, he recovered the city in 1390 and ruled it until 1405, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> captured Padua after a long war. Taken to Venice as a prisoner, he was strangled in prison in 1406 together with two of his sons, ending the dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francesco-novello-da-carrara.jpg" alt="Francesco Novello da Carrara" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified medallist · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paduan_14th_Century,_Francesco_II_da_Carrara,_1359-1406,_Lord_of_Padua_1388-1405_(obverse),_1390,_NGA_135987.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
